            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>1. Monitor the Right Balance Metrics</h2>
<p>The most common mistake in balance monitoring is relying on the total amount recharged while ignoring the funds that are actually available for delivery. Before scaling campaigns, create a balance sheet for every ad account and record the following:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Total recharge amount</strong>: used for reconciliation with historical payments.</li>
<li><strong>Current available balance</strong>: use the spendable balance displayed in the advertising platform.</li>
<li><strong>Pending or restricted funds</strong>: some platforms temporarily reserve amounts for unsettled spend, rejected payments, or ongoing reviews.</li>
<li><strong>Average daily spend over the last three days</strong>: used to estimate how long the account can continue running.</li>
<li><strong>Next scheduled debit date</strong>: this helps identify cash-flow risks before a clustered billing date.</li>
</ul>
<p>Set tiered alerts based on consumption speed. For example, a balance that can support less than seven days of delivery may trigger a yellow alert, while less than three days may trigger a red alert. Increase the alert level when a campaign is being scaled or a large recharge is planned. These thresholds are not universal; adjust them according to customer value, conversion windows, billing schedules, and cash flow.</p>
<h2>2. Automate Alerts and Run a Daily Checklist</h2>
<h3>Daily checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Check the available balance in every account instead of reviewing only recharge records.</li>
<li>[ ] Compare yesterday’s spend with the three-day average to detect unexpected scaling.</li>
<li>[ ] Verify that payment methods are active, valid, and not awaiting additional verification.</li>
<li>[ ] Review budget pacing so that one campaign does not consume the entire account balance.</li>
<li>[ ] Send yellow and red alerts through email, Slack, Feishu, WeCom, or another team channel.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each alert should include the account name, platform, available balance, estimated days of runway, previous-day spend, responsible owner, recharge deadline, and backup payment method. For high-spend accounts, consider checking the balance at least twice a day.</p>
<p>A balance threshold alone is not enough. If daily consumption suddenly doubles, trigger an alert even when the remaining balance appears comfortable. The warning should reflect both the amount available and the speed at which it is being spent.</p>
<h2>3. Run a Balance Stress Test Before Scaling</h2>
<p>Before increasing budgets, duplicating campaigns, or launching several creatives at once, complete the following steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Estimate spend for the next three to seven days using recent actual consumption and leaving a reasonable buffer.</li>
<li>Confirm that available funds cover expected spend, platform fees, and pending charges.</li>
<li>Identify which limit will be reached first: the campaign budget or the account balance.</li>
<li>Confirm that automatic recharge is enabled and that failed payments trigger an immediate notification.</li>
<li>Prepare a compliant backup payment method, without switching cards before confirming authorization and transaction limits.</li>
<li>Adjust high-spend and low-spend campaigns in separate steps to avoid several campaigns competing for the same balance simultaneously.</li>
</ol>
<p>Consider an example from a utility App team. During a creative scale-up, the account showed a substantial balance, but part of it was still pending settlement. The actual available amount was lower than the initial estimate. The team added available balance and projected consumption to its daily checklist and reserved a buffer before changing budgets. This prevented a late-night campaign slowdown.</p>
<h2>4. Prepare a Contingency Process for Failed Payments</h2>
<p>When an alert is triggered, the team should not simply recharge. It should confirm whether the funds have become available:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check the transaction status</strong>. Determine whether the payment is processing, declined, restricted, or delayed in synchronization.</li>
<li><strong>Pause nonessential expansion</strong>. Keep proven campaigns running while reducing new budget increases and campaign duplication.</li>
<li><strong>Use a compliant backup payment method</strong>. Confirm its limits and settlement time.</li>
<li><strong>Review delivery continuity</strong>. Check for budget limits, campaign delays, or learning-stage resets.</li>
<li><strong>Reconcile after recovery</strong>. Match the debit amount, account, and platform records before restoring budgets gradually.</li>
</ol>
<h2>5. Use a Central Dashboard for Multiple Accounts</h2>
<p>For teams managing several accounts, maintain a master table containing the account, owner, payment method, balance, consumption, and alert status. Establish a few controls:</p>
<ul>
<li>Set separate thresholds for primary and testing accounts.</li>
<li>Review high-spend accounts at least twice each day.</li>
<li>Transfer alert recipients and payment permissions when ownership changes.</li>
<li>Reconcile recharges, platform charges, account balances, and finance statements every month.</li>
<li>Do not treat a successful recharge as proof that delivery is healthy. Confirm the platform’s available balance and advertising delivery status.</li>
</ul>
<p>The objective is not to keep every balance excessively high. It is to ensure that, before every scale-up, the team knows how long the funds will last, who will take action, and whether a backup solution is ready. In this way, balance alerts become an operational safeguard for continuous advertising rather than a last-minute recharge reminder.</p>
